\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
In $\mathbb C^6$, two orthonormal bases are mutually unbiased if the absolute value of every cross-basis inner product is $1/\sqrt6$. What is the largest number of bases that are pairwise mutually unbiased?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://afonsobandeira.wordpress.com/2015/12/07/10l42pcompressedsensing/}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
In $\mathbb C^6$, two orthonormal bases are mutually unbiased if the absolute value of every cross-basis inner product is $1/\sqrt6$. What is the largest number of bases that are pairwise mutually unbiased?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2800602 (AMR-027-0602). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://afonsobandeira.wordpress.com/2015/12/07/10l42pcompressedsensing/}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:2800602}

